WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
We're going to start today with something that&nbsp;
I know some in my audience don't really want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.120
accept, but when we deny an&nbsp;
empirical reality, we're really all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.800
the worse off. There's no advantage to denying&nbsp;
an empirical reality, and the empirical reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:26.160
is that Joe Biden's approval rating is not good,&nbsp;
and it's actually getting down to where Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.800
Trump was at this point in his presidency. And&nbsp;
we're going to talk about why we'll talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:37.040
the implications and we'll talk about what could&nbsp;
be done to reverse it. But it is now very clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.560
that Joe Biden has a political problem on his&nbsp;
hands, and as a result, Democrats have a political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:50.640
problem on their hands. In terms of the numbers,&nbsp;
Joe Biden's approval has dropped below 40 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.360
for the first time in his presidency, according&nbsp;
to the real clear politics average of polls. Now

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.800
what real clear politics does is it takes a number&nbsp;
of different polls individually, some of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:12.080
might have a bias. Like, for example, it includes&nbsp;
the Rasmussen poll, which can have sort of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:19.200
right wing bias. It includes the IBD Tipp poll,&nbsp;
which some say has a bit of a left wing bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:26.080
etc. And you get an average. And yesterday that&nbsp;
average dropped for the first time below 40 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:33.040
thirty nine point eight, and today has recovered&nbsp;
just a sliver to exactly 40. And as you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.880
if you're watching, that's the lowest that&nbsp;
it has been since the start of Joe Biden's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.920
presidency. And if we ask the question,&nbsp;
well, it's common that sometimes presidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.840
come into office with a higher approval&nbsp;
and then it sort of declines over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.840
How does it compare to past presidents?&nbsp;
And FiveThirtyEight does that for us. And

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:04.400
what you see when you look is that Joe Biden came&nbsp;
into office with a significantly higher approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.680
than Donald Trump, about 10 points higher. And&nbsp;
that has slowly gone down, down down to where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.280
they are now, almost identical at this point&nbsp;
in their presidencies. Biden and Trump are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.440
basically equal in terms of approval. Joe Biden's&nbsp;
approval is well below where Barack Obama's was at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:28.560
this point in his presidency. It, because of 911,&nbsp;
is dramatically below where George W. Bush's was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.280
although George W. Bush did ultimately squander&nbsp;
the high approval that came in the aftermath of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.280
911. Joe Biden's approval, although for a period&nbsp;
of time, was higher than Bill Clinton's. At this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.560
point in his presidency, it has now dropped&nbsp;
below. Biden's approval is well below where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.520
George H.W. Bush's was. It is below where&nbsp;
Ronald Reagan's was. And if we want to go

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.040
back further, it is also below where&nbsp;
Jimmy Carter's was similar to Gerald Ford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.000
well below Richard Nixon. And you know, we could&nbsp;
go back further and and it's a different era if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.560
you go back to LBJ, Kennedy and Eisenhower. It's&nbsp;
a completely different political era altogether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.320
I don't know how much we get from looking at those&nbsp;
numbers, but the point here is Joe Biden does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:18.000
a political problem on his hands now. Why is Joe&nbsp;
Biden's approval so low? It's a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:25.680
things. First and foremost, it has been some time&nbsp;
since Joe Biden has had what we might consider a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:33.200
major accomplishment. Relatively early on, Joe&nbsp;
Biden had the COVID stimulus package. Shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.920
after that, there was the dramatic rollout of the&nbsp;
accelerated rollout of COVID vaccines. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.560
Joe Biden got hit with the Delta variant.&nbsp;
Then Joe Biden left Afghanistan, which I

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.840
believe is 110 percent the right decision.&nbsp;
But the right was able to weaponize it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.240
pretty successfully in terms of saying it&nbsp;
was chaotic and mis planned, and it said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.240
all the things that it would have been&nbsp;
under Trump as well. But that hurt Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.880
infrastructure was done. But it got&nbsp;
relatively little coverage. And then now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.800
there's sort of this lull. And so I think there's&nbsp;
a couple of different questions that we could ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.040
one in a vacuum. What could Joe Biden do to&nbsp;
raise his approval in the short term? I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.560
a tick tock clip about this by by popular demand.&nbsp;
And there's a few different things that Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.840
could do in the short term, although I don't&nbsp;
know how much they would benefit him long term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.440
You know, we've talked about the student loan&nbsp;
forgiveness. There is no doubt that doing some

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.280
student loan forgiveness would help Joe Biden's&nbsp;
approval rating in the immediate. But would it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.440
really help him? And would it help Democrats&nbsp;
longer term? Maybe, maybe not. I'm not sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.560
something on decriminalizing marijuana federally,&nbsp;
I think would be very popular. But could he get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:52.480
it done? Is sort of a real question. What about&nbsp;
announcing that the pandemic is over and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.240
question, which just three days&nbsp;
ago was sort of like, Yeah, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.560
it is very clear that. Due to political pressure&nbsp;
and, quite frankly, fatigue with the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.120
numerous states are now saying we're getting rid&nbsp;
of masks, we're getting rid of masks in schools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.360
we're getting rid of, in some&nbsp;
cases, requirements for vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.560
to go to certain types of businesses.&nbsp;
And my expectation is that for political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.920
reasons between now and March 1st, which&nbsp;
is the date of the next state of the Union

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.880
that Joe Biden is going to give, I expect it could&nbsp;
be next week. It could be on the day of the State&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:33.200
of the Union sometime between now and March 1st.&nbsp;
I expect the Biden White House to come out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.920
say we are now at the point where life can really&nbsp;
start going back to normal now. There's divergent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.840
views as I talk to you about yesterday as to&nbsp;
whether that is a scientifically and medically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.520
sound thing to do. Some say there's really no&nbsp;
more milestones to wait for. We don't get a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.720
deadly variant. It's sort of going to be like this&nbsp;
for the next, however many years. There's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.880
nothing else to wait for. It's time to go back&nbsp;
to a quote normal and let people make decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.400
for themselves about their level of risk. The&nbsp;
counterpoint to that is that there's still a

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.280
lot of people dying as a result of the&nbsp;
surge that started a couple of months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.360
ago. It is not the time to do it.&nbsp;
I think that there is significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.280
leaning right now within the Biden White&nbsp;
House to sometime between now and March 1st,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:21.040
take a sort of mission accomplished approach,&nbsp;
make a mission accomplished type statement when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.440
it comes to COVID, whether it will end up being&nbsp;
the right thing or not, I don't know. But Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.200
Biden does have a political problem on his hands,&nbsp;
and these are some of the things certainly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.840
when it comes to COVID are are being considered&nbsp;
and very quickly because we're basically eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.280
and a half months from the midterms. What happens&nbsp;
in the next six weeks is going to set the tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.080
for what the midterm races are going to&nbsp;
look like in terms of what Republicans will

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.920
use to run against Democrats. The culture stuff&nbsp;
for sure. But what else are they going to use?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.440
And there's this limited window&nbsp;
now to set the tone for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.800
for what that's going to be. And of&nbsp;
course, we'll be following what it is.

